"reference request - What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit? - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"lineno\":160}); Total lines: 381\nL132: The case of the \"straight polyominos\" proposed by Saúl is the digital equivalent of the celebrated Kakeya problem: 【27†en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kakeya_set†en.wikipedia.org】\nL133: \nL134: –  【28†domotorp】\nL135: \nL136: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 5:13\nL137: \nL138:   * 2\nL139: @a3nm Take a nice set $S$ whose area is $<0.01n^2$ and contains a length $n$ segment in every direction. Such a set $S$ exists by the constructions given on Wikipeida for the Kakeya set. Now pixelize $S$ by checking which unit squares it intersects from the unit grid. The number of these squares is $0.01n^2 +O(n)$ as the perimeter of $S$ is nice. As $n\\to \\infty$, this gives an upper bound of $0.01n^2$, and we can pick $0.01$ as small as we want to.\nL140: \nL141: –  【28†domotorp】\nL142: \nL143: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 8:53\nL144: \nL145:   * 1\nL146: I've asked about something similar about free polyominoes on 【29†Code Golf Stack Exchange†codegolf.stackexchange.com】 and 【30†Math Stack Exchange†math.stackexchange.com】.\nL147: \nL148: –  【31†Peter Kagey】\nL149: \nL150: Commented Apr 24, 2022 at 2:11\nL151: \nL152:  |  【32†Show 17 more comments】\nL153: ## 3 Answers\nL154: \nL155: Sorted by:\nL156: \nL157: [Select]\nL158: \nL159: [Input]\nL160: \nL161: 9\nL162: \nL163: [Input]\nL164: It is actually $\\ge cn^2$ with some $c>0$. The value of $c$ I'll obtain is pretty dismal but I tried to trade the precision for the argument simplicity everywhere I could, so it can be certainly improved quite a bit. I have no doubt that it is written somewhere (perhaps, in the continuous form: the $2$-dimensional measure of a set containing a shift of every rectifiable curve of length $1$ is at least some positive constant) but I'll leave it to more educated people to provide the reference.\nL165: We shall work on the 2D $n\\times n$ lattice torus $T$ whose size $n$ is a power of $2$. Clearly, wrapping around makes the set only smaller. Define $K$ to be the integer such that $2^{K^3+K}\\le n< 2^{(K+1)^3+K+1}$ (I assume that $n$ is large enough, so $K$ is not too small either).\nL166: \nL167: Put $\\varepsilon_k=2^{-k}, M_k=2^{k^3}$ ($k\\ge 4$). Note that $\\frac 12+3\\sum_{k\\ge 4}\\varepsilon_k=\\frac 78<1$. Put $\\mu_k=\\frac 12+3\\sum_{m=4}^{k}\\varepsilon_m$ (so $\\mu_3=\\frac 12$).\nL168: Now take any set $E\\subset T$ of density $d(E,T)=\\frac{|E|}{|T|}=1/2$. Our aim will be to construct a connected set $P$ of cardinality $|P|\\le Cn$ such that no its lattice shift of $E$ on $T$ contains $P$.\nL169: Start with dividing $T$ into $M_4^2$ equal squares $Q_4$. Notice that the portion of squares $Q_4$ with density $d(E,Q_4)=\\frac{|E\\cap Q_4|}{|Q_4|}>\\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$ is at most $\\mu_3/(\\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4)\\le 1-\\varepsilon_4$. Now choose $N_4=\\frac{2\\log_2 (M_4/\\varepsilon_4)}{\\varepsilon_4}=2\\cdot(4^3+4)\\cdot 2^4$ squares $Q_4$ independently at random.\nL170: The probability that none of them has density $d(E,Q_4)\\le \\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$ is at most $(1-\\varepsilon_4)^{N_4}< \\left(\\frac{\\varepsilon_4}{M_4}\\right)^2$. This means that if we consider not only the standard partition but also all its shifts $E'$ by multiples of $\\varepsilon_4 n/M_4$, then there exists a configuration $P_4$ of $N_4$ squares $Q_4$ such that for each such shift, the density of $E'$ in at least one square $Q_4$ in $P_4$ is $d(E',Q_4)\\le \\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"transpos\"}); Total lines: 2664\nNo matching text found for \"transpos\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"pattern\":\"Generate(\"}); Total lines: 2664\nL244:     ..##.......\nL245: \nL246:     ...#######..\nL247:     ...#########\nL248:     ############\nL249:     ..#####....#\nL250:     ..#####.....\nL251:     ...####.....\nL252:     `\nL253: To find 43 for n=12 took a bit over 7.5 hours.\nL254: ### Polyomino code\nL255: \nL256:     `using MiniSAT;\nL257:     using System;\nL258:     using System.Collections.Generic;\nL259:     using System.Diagnostics;\nL260:     using System.Linq;\nL261: \nL262:     namespace PPCG167484\nL263:     {\nL264:         internal class SatGenerator\nL265:         {\nL266:             public static void Main()\nL267:             {\nL268:                 for (int n = 1; n < 13; n++)\nL269:                 {\nL270:                     int width = n;\nL271:                     int height = (n + 1) >> 1;\nL272:                     var polys = FreePolyomino.All(n);\nL273:                     (var solver, var unaryWeights) = Generate(polys, width, height);\nL274: \nL275:                     int previous = width * height + 1;\nL276:                     while (true)\nL277:                     {\nL278:                         Stopwatch sw = new Stopwatch(); sw.Start();\nL279:                         if (solver.Solve())\nL280:                         {\nL281:                             // The weight of the solution might be smaller than the target\nL282:                             int weight = Enumerable.Range(0, width * height).Count(x => solver.Model[x] == Solver.l_True);\nL283: \nL284:                             Console.Write($\"{n}\\t<={weight}\\t{sw.Elapsed.TotalSeconds:F3}s\\t\");\nL285:                             int cell = 0;\nL286:                             for (int y = 0; y < height; y++)\nL287:                             {\nL288:                                 if (y > 0) Console.Write('_');\nL289:                                 for (int x = 0; x < width; x++) Console.Write(solver.Model[cell++] == Solver.l_True ? '#' : '.');\nL290:                             }\nL291:                             Console.WriteLine();\nL292: \nL293:                             // Now knock out that weight\nL294:                             for (int i = weight - 1; i < previous - 1; i++) solver.AddClause(~unaryWeights[i]);\nL295:                             previous = weight;\nL296:                         }\nL297:                         else\nL298:                         {\nL299:                             Console.WriteLine(\"--------\");\nL300:                             break;\nL301:                         }\nL302:                     }\nL303:                 }\nL304:             }\nL305: \nL306:             public static Tuple<Solver, Solver.Lit[]> Generate(IEnumerable<FreePolyomino> polys, int width, int height)\nL307:             {\nL308:                 var solver = new Solver();\nL309: \nL310:                 if (width == 12) solver.Prealloc(6037071 + 448, 72507588 + 6008); // HACK!\nL311:                 // Variables: 0 to width * height - 1 are the cells available to fill.\nL312:                 for (int i = 0; i < width * height; i++) solver.NewVar();\nL313: \nL314:                 foreach (var poly in polys)\nL315:                 {\nL316:                     // We naturally get a DNF: each position of each orientation is a conjunction of poly.Weight variables,\nL317:                     // and we require any one. Therefore we add an auxiliary variable per.\nL318: \nL319:                     var polyAuxs = new List<Solver.Lit>();\nL320:                     foreach (var orientation in poly.OrientedPolyominos)\nL321:                     {\nL322:                         int maxh = height;\nL323:                         // Optimisation: break symmetry\nL324:                         if (orientation.BBHeight == 1) maxh = ((height + 1) >> 1);\nL325: \nL326:                         for (int dy = 0; dy + orientation.BBHeight <= maxh; dy++)\nL327:                         {\nL328:                             for (int dx = 0; dx + orientation.BBWidth <= width; dx++)\nL329:                             {\nL330:                                 var currentAux = solver.NewVar();\nL331:                                 for (int y = 0; y < orientation.BBHeight; y++)\nL332:                                 {\nL333:                                     uint tmp = orientation.Rows[y];\nL334:                                     for (int x = 0; tmp > 0; x++, tmp >>= 1)\nL335:                                     {\nL336:                                         if ((tmp & 1) == 1) solver.AddClause(~currentAux, new Solver.Lit((y + dy) * width + x + dx));\nL337:                                     }\nL338:                                 }\nL339: \nL340:                                 polyAuxs.Add(currentAux);\nL341:                             }\nL342:                         }\nL343:                     }\nL344:                     solver.AddClause(polyAuxs.ToArray());\nL345:                 }\nL346: \nL347:                 // Efficient CNF encoding of Boolean cardinality constraints, Bailleux and Boufkhad, http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.458.7676&rep=rep1&type=pdf\nL348:                 var unaryWeights = _BBSum(0, width * height, solver);\nL349:                 return Tuple.Create(solver, unaryWeights);\nL350:             }\nL351: \nL352:             private static Solver.Lit[] _BBSum(int from, int num, Solver solver)\nL353:             {\nL354:                 var sum = new Solver.Lit[num];\nL355:                 if (num == 1) sum[0] = new Solver.Lit(from);\nL356:                 else\nL357:                 {\nL358:                     var left = _BBSum(from, num >> 1, solver);\nL1145:                 List<LBool> seen = AnalyzeSeen;\nL1146:                 int pathC = 0;\nL1147:                 Lit p = lit_Undef;\nL1148: \nL1149:                 // Generate conflict clause\nL1150:                 out_learnt.Push(lit_Undef); // (placeholder for the asserting literal)\nL1151:                 var out_btlevel = 0;\nL1152:                 int index = Trail.Count - 1;\nL1153:                 do\nL1154:                 {\nL1155:                     Assert(confl != null); // (otherwise should be UIP)\nL1156: \nL1157:                     if (confl.IsLearnt) ClaBumpActivity(confl);\nL1158: \nL1159:                     for (int j = (p == lit_Undef) ? 0 : 1; j < confl.Size; j++)\nL1160:                     {\nL1161:                         Lit q = confl[j];\nL1162:                         var v = q.Var;\nL1163:                         if (seen[v] == l_Undef && Level[v] > 0)\nL1164:                         {\nL1165:                             VarBumpActivity(q);\nL1166:                             seen[v] = l_True;\nL1167:                             if (Level[v] == DecisionLevel) pathC++;\nL1168:                             else\nL1169:                             {\nL1644:     -                ws.ShrinkTo(j);\nL1645:                  }\nL1646: \nL1647:                  return confl;\nL1648:     @@ -792,8 +850,10 @@ namespace MiniSAT\nL1649:                  for (int i = SimpDBAssigns; i < nAssigns; i++)\nL1650:                  {\nL1651:                      Lit p = Trail[i];\nL1652:     -                Watches[p.Index].Clear();\nL1653:     -                Watches[(~p).Index].Clear();\nL1654:     +                Watches[p.Index] = null;\nL1655:     +                Watches[(~p).Index] = null;\nL1656:     +                BinaryWatches[p.Index] = null;\nL1657:     +                BinaryWatches[(~p).Index] = null;\nL1658:                  }\nL1659: \nL1660:                  // Remove satisfied clauses:\nL1661:     `\nL1662: ### Distinct solutions\nL1663: Counting solutions to a SAT problem is straightforward, if sometimes slow: you find a solution, add a new clause which directly rules it out, and run again. Here it's easy to generate the equivalence class of solutions under the symmetries of the rectangle, so the following code suffices to generate all distinct solutions.\nL1664: \nL1665:     `                // Force it to the known optimal weight\nL1666:                     for (int i = optimal[n]; i < unaryWeights.Length; i++) solver.AddClause(~unaryWeights[i]);\nL1667:                     while (solver.Solve())\nL1668:                     {\nL1669:                         var rows = new uint[height];\nL1670:                         int cell = 0;\nL1671:                         for (int y = 0; y < height; y++)\nL1672:                         {\nL1673:                             for (int x = 0; x < width; x++)\nL1674:                             {\nL1675:                                 if (solver.Model[cell++] == Solver.l_True) rows[y] |= 1U << x;\nL1676:                             }\nL1677:                         }\nL1678:                         var poly = new FreePolyomino(new OrientedPolyomino(rows));\nL1679:                         Console.WriteLine(poly.CanonicalOrientation);\nL1680: \nL1681:                         foreach (var orientation in poly.OrientedPolyominos)\nL1682:                         {\nL1683:                             if (orientation.BBWidth != width || orientation.BBHeight != height) continue;\nL1684: \nL1685:                             // Exclude it\nL1686:                             List<Solver.Lit> soln = new List<Solver.Lit>(previous);\nL1687:                             cell = 0;\nL1688:                             for (int y = 0; y < height; y++)\nL1689:                             {\nL1690:                                 uint row = orientation.Rows[y];\nL1691:                                 for (int x = 0; x < width; x++, cell++)\nL1692:                                 {\nL1693:                                     if ((row & 1) == 1) soln.Add(~new Solver.Lit(cell));\nL1694:                                     row >>= 1;\nL1695:                                 }\nL1696:                             }\nL1697:                             solver.AddClause(soln.ToArray());\nL1698:                         }\nL1699:                     }\nL1700:     `\nL1701: This may be useful for people to generate or test hypotheses about the \"typical\" structure which can guide searches for higher \\$n\\$.\nL1702: \nL1703:     `100_111\nL1704:     010_111\nL1705: \nL1706:     0110_1111\nL1707:     1100_1111\nL1708: \nL1709:     01000_11111_01110\nL1710:     00100_11111_11100\nL1711: \nL1712:     011000_111111_011110\nL1713:     110000_111111_111100\nL1714:     011000_011110_111111\nL1715:     001100_111100_111111\nL1716:     110000_111100_111111\nL1717:     001100_111111_111100\nL1718: \nL1719:     0010000_0111100_0111110_1111111\nL1720:     0001000_1111000_1111111_1111100\nL1721:     0001000_0111000_1111111_1111110\nL1722:     0100000_1111000_1111111_1111100\nL1723:     0100000_1111000_1111100_1111111\nL1724:     0001000_0111000_1111110_1111111\nL1725:     0001000_0111110_1111111_1111000\nL1726:     0100000_1111000_0111110_1111111\nL1727:     0001000_1111100_1111111_1111000\nL1728:     1100000_1110000_1111100_1111111\nL1729:     0100000_1111111_1111100_0111100\nL1730:     0011000_0111000_0111110_1111111\nL1731:     1010000_1110000_1111100_1111111\nL1732:     0011000_1110000_1111100_1111111\nL1733:     0010100_0011100_1111100_1111111\nL