",\"pattern\":\"37\"}); Total lines: 2664\nL26: # 【15†Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes】\nL27: \nL28: 【16†Ask Question】\nL29: \nL30: Asked 8 years, 4 months ago\nL31: \nL32: Modified 【17†6 years ago】\nL33: \nL34: Viewed 1k times\nL35: \nL36: [Input]\nL37: \nL38: 30\nL39: \nL40: [Input]\nL41: \nL42: On Math Stack Exchange, I asked a question about the 【18†smallest region that can contain all free n-ominos†math.stackexchange.com】.\nL43: \nL44: I'd like to add this sequence to the 【19†On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences†oeis.org】 once I have more terms.\nL45: # Example\nL46: \nL47: A nine-cell region is the smallest subset of the plane that can contain all twelve free 【20†5-ominoes†en.wikipedia.org】, as illustrated below. (A free polyomino is one that can be rotated and flipped.)\nL48: \nL49: (A twelve-cell region is the smallest subset of the plane the can contain all 35 free 【21†6-ominoes†en.wikipedia.org】.)\nL50: \nL51: * * *\nL52: # The Challenge\nL53: \nL54: Compute upper bounds on the smallest regions of the plane that can contain all n-ominoes as a function of n.\nL55: \nL56: Such a table begins:\nL57: \nL58:     `n | size\nL59:     --+-------\nL60:     1 | 1*\nL61:     2 | 2*\nL62:     3 | 4*\nL63:     4 | 6*\nL64:     5 | 9*\nL65:     6 | 12*\nL66:     7 | 37\nL67:     8 | 50\nL68:     9 | 65\nL69: \nL70:     *These values are the smallest possible.\nL71:     `\nL72: \nL73: * * *\nL74: # Example submission\nL75: \nL76:     `1-omino: 1\nL77:     #\nL78: \nL79:     2-omino: 2\nL80:     ##\nL81: \nL82:     3-omino: 4\nL83:     ###\nL84:      #\nL85: \nL86:     4-omino: 6\nL87:     ####\nL88:      ##\nL89: \nL90:     5-omino: 9\nL91:      #\nL92:     #####\nL93:      ###\nL94: \nL95:     6-omino: 12\nL96:     ####\nL97:     ######\nL98:       ##\nL99: \nL100:     7-omino: <= 37\nL101:     #######\nL102:     ######\nL103:     ######\nL104:     ######\nL105:     ######\nL106:     ######\nL107:     `\nL108: \nL109: * * *\nL110: # Scoring\nL111: \nL112: Run your program for as long as you'd like, and post your list of upper bounds together with the shapes that achieve each.\nL113: \nL114: The winner will be the participant whose table is lexicographically the earliest (with \"infinity\" appended to the shorter submissions.) If two participants submit the same results, then the earlier submission wins.\nL115: \nL116: For example, if the submissions are\nL117: \nL118:     `Aliyah: [1,2,4,6,12,30,50]\nL119:     Brian:  [1,2,4,6,12,37,49,65]\nL120:     Clare:  [1,2,4,6,12,30]\nL121:     `\nL122: then Aliyah wins. She beats Brian because 30 < 37, and she beats Clare because 50 < infinity.\nL123: \nL124:   * 【22†code-challenge】\nL125:   * 【23†sequence】\nL126:   * 【24†geometry】\nL127:   * 【25†combinatorics】\nL128:   * 【26†polyomino】\nL129: \nL130: 【27†Improve this question】\nL131: \nL132: Follow [Input]\nL133: \nL134: 【28†edited Jun 28, 2018 at 19:48】\nL135: \nL136: asked Jun 26, 2018 at 18:02\nL137: \nL138: 【29†Peter Kagey】\nL139: \nL140: 8,385 1 1 gold badge 29 29 silver badges 122 122 bronze badges\nL141: \nL142:   * 2\nL143: \nL144: Interesting challenge, I will work on this later today!\nL145: \nL146: –  【30†orlp】\nL147: \nL148: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 18:23\nL149: \nL150:   * 1\nL151: Polyominos with or without holes?\nL152: \nL153: –  【31†Peter Taylor】\nL154: \nL155: Commented Jun 27, 2018 at 6:43\nL156: \nL157:   * Do programs have to run deterministically?\nL158: \nL159: –  【32†N. Virgo】\nL160: \nL161: Commented Jun 27, 2018 at 14:09\nL162: \nL163:   * 1\nL164: \nL165: @PeterTaylor, polyominoes with holes—but my naïve expectation is that it wouldn't change the result.\nL166: \nL167: –  【29†Peter Kagey】\nL168: \nL169: Commented Jun 27, 2018 at 16:06\nL170: \nL171:   * 1\nL172: Technically, I think you're only interested in lattice aligned placements of the polyominoes, right? You can't rotate the piece by 23.42132 degrees, right?\nL173: \nL174: –  【33†izzyg】\nL175: \nL176: Commented Jun 29, 2018 at 6:13\nL177: \nL178:  |  【34†Show 2 more comments】\nL179: ## 4 Answers\nL180: \nL181: Sorted by:\nL182: \nL183: [Select]\nL184: \nL185: [Input]\nL186: \nL187: 11\nL188: \nL189: [Input]\nL190: \nL191: +150\nL192: ## C# and SAT: 1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 12, 17, 20, 26, 31, 37, 43\nL193: \nL194: If we limit the bounding box, there is a fairly obvious expression of the problem in terms of 【35†SAT†en.wikipedia.org】: each translation of each orientation of each free polyomino is a large conjunction; for each polyomino we form a disjunction over its conjunctions; and then we require each disjunction to be true and the total number of cells used to be limited.\nL195: To limit the number of cells my initial version built a full adder; then I used bitonic sort for unary counting (similar to 【36†this earlier answer】 but generalised); finally I settled on the approach described by Bailleux and Boufkhad in 【37†Efficient CNF encoding of Boolean cardinality constraints†citeseerx.ist.psu.edu】.\nL196: I wanted to make the post self-contained, so I dug out a C# implementation of a SAT solver with a BSD licence which was state-of-the-art about 15 years ago, replaced its NIH list implementation with `System.Collections.Generic.List<T>` (gaining a factor of 2 in speed), golfed it from 50kB down to 31kB to fit in the 64kB post limit, and then did some aggressive work on reducing memory usage. This code can obviously be adapted to output a DIMACS file which can then be passed to more modern solvers.\nL197: ## Solutions found\nL198: \nL199:     `#\nL200: \nL201:     ##\nL202: \nL203:     ###\nL204:     ..#\nL205: \nL206:     ####\nL207:     .##.\nL208: \nL209:     ..#..\nL210:     #####\nL211:     ..###\nL212: \nL213:     .####.\nL214:     ######\nL215:     .##...\nL216: \nL217:     ....#..\nL218:     #######\nL219:     #####..\nL220:     .####..\nL221: \nL222:     ########\nL223:     ..######\nL224:     .....###\nL225:     .....###\nL226: \nL227:     #########\nL228:     #######..\nL229:     ..#####..\nL230:     ....##...\nL231:     ....###..\nL232: \nL233:     ##########\nL234:     ########..\nL235:     ..######..\nL236:     ....####..\nL237:     .....###..\nL238: \nL239:     ..#######..\nL240:     ..#########\nL241:     ###########\nL242:     ..####.....\nL243:     ..####.....\nL244:     ..##.......\nL245: \nL246:     ...#######..\nL247:     ...#########\nL248:     ############\nL249:     ..#####....#\nL250:     ..#####.....\nL251:     ...####.....\nL252:     `\nL253: To find 43 for n=12 took a bit over 7.5 hours.\nL254: ### Polyomino code\nL255: \nL256:     `using MiniSAT;\nL257:     using System;\nL258:     using System.Collections.Generic;\nL259:     using System.Diagnostics;\nL260:     using System.Linq;\nL261: \nL262:     namespace PPCG167484\nL263:     {\nL264:         internal class SatGenerator\nL265:         {\nL266:             public static void Main()\nL267:             {\nL268:                 for (int n = 1; n < 13; n++)\nL269:                 {\nL270:                     int width = n;\nL271:                     int height = (n + 1) >> 1;\nL272:                     var polys = FreePolyomino.All(n);\nL273:                     (var solver, var unaryWeights) = Generate(polys, width, height);\nL274: \nL275:                     int previous = width * height + 1;\nL276:                     while (true)\nL277:                     {\nL278:                         Stopwatch sw = new Stopwatch(); sw.Start();\nL279:                         if (solver.Solve())\nL280:                         {\nL281:                             // The weight of the solution might be smaller than the target\nL507:             public bool Equals(OrientedPolyomino other) => CompareTo(other) == 0;\nL508:             public override int GetHashCode() => Rows.Aggregate(0, (h, row) => h * 37 + (int)row);\nL509:             public override bool Equals(object obj) => (obj is OrientedPolyomino other) && Equals(other);\nL510:             public override string ToString()\nL511:             {\nL512:                 var width = BBWidth;\nL513:                 return string.Join(\"_\", Rows.Select(row => Helper.ToString(row, width)));\nL514:             }\nL2225:                 if (BBHeight != other.BBHeight) return BBHeight.CompareTo(other.BBHeight);\nL2226: \nL2227:                 for (int i = 0; i < BBHeight; i++)\nL2228:                 {\nL2229:                     if (Rows[i] != other.Rows[i]) return Rows[i].CompareTo(other.Rows[i]);\nL2230:                 }\nL2231: \nL2232:                 return 0;\nL2233:             }\nL2234:             public bool Equals(OrientedPolyomino other) => CompareTo(other) == 0;\nL2235:             public override int GetHashCode() => Rows.Aggregate(0, (h, row) => h * 37 + (int)row);\n"
